/* Exam paper B: 100 questions. Same format as exam-a.js. */
window.EXAM_PAPERS=window.EXAM_PAPERS||{};
EXAM_PAPERS.B={title:'Paper B',blurb:'A second full paper on the same blocks with different scenarios, more data interpretation and more drug questions.',qs:[
/* ---------- haemostasis ---------- */
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 5-year-old boy has a swollen, hot knee after a minor fall. APTT 62 s (reference 25–35), PT normal, platelets normal. A 50:50 mix with normal plasma corrects the APTT. Factor VIII activity is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
 o:['Factor IX deficiency (haemophilia B)','Factor VIII deficiency (haemophilia A)','Von Willebrand disease','Factor XII deficiency','A lupus anticoagulant'],a:0,
 e:'Correction on mixing means a missing factor rather than an inhibitor. A joint bleed in a boy with a long APTT and normal VIII points to haemophilia B (Christmas disease), confirmed by a low factor IX assay. Factor XII deficiency also lengthens the APTT but never causes bleeding. A lupus anticoagulant would not correct on mixing and causes clotting, not bleeding.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 29-year-old woman has had two second-trimester miscarriages and now has a DVT. Her APTT is prolonged and does not correct when mixed with normal plasma. Platelets are 120 ×10⁹/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
 o:['Antiphospholipid syndrome','Haemophilia A carrier','Von Willebrand disease','Factor V Leiden','Vitamin K deficiency'],a:0,
 e:'Antiphospholipid antibodies (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, anti-β2-glycoprotein I) interfere with phospholipid-dependent clotting tests in the tube, prolonging the APTT without correction on mixing, yet in the body they cause arterial and venous thrombosis, pregnancy loss and mild thrombocytopenia: the "lupus anticoagulant paradox". Factor V Leiden does not prolong the APTT.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 64-year-old has a coronary stent and takes aspirin and clopidogrel. How does clopidogrel reduce stent thrombosis?',
 o:['Its active metabolite irreversibly blocks the platelet P2Y₁₂ ADP receptor','It irreversibly acetylates platelet COX-1','It blocks GPIIb/IIIa so fibrinogen cannot bridge platelets','It directly inhibits thrombin','It inhibits phosphodiesterase and raises platelet cAMP'],a:0,
 e:'Clopidogrel is a prodrug activated by CYP2C19 (poor metabolisers respond less, and omeprazole may reduce activation). Blocking P2Y₁₂ stops ADP from switching on GPIIb/IIIa, reducing aggregation for the platelet\'s lifespan. Combined with aspirin (COX-1/TXA₂) it is dual antiplatelet therapy. Dipyridamole works through phosphodiesterase; tirofiban blocks GPIIb/IIIa.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 78-year-old on warfarin for atrial fibrillation presents with a large upper GI bleed and a pulse of 120. INR is 8.2. Besides resuscitation and stopping warfarin, what is the most appropriate reversal?',
 o:['IV vitamin K plus prothrombin complex concentrate','Oral vitamin K alone','Protamine sulfate','Tranexamic acid alone','Idarucizumab'],a:0,
 e:'Major bleeding on warfarin needs immediate replacement of factors II, VII, IX and X: prothrombin complex concentrate works within minutes. IV vitamin K (5 mg) restarts the liver\'s own production over 6–24 hours, so both are needed. FFP is a less effective alternative if PCC is unavailable. Protamine reverses heparin; idarucizumab reverses dabigatran.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 4-year-old has widespread petechiae and bruises two weeks after a viral illness. He is otherwise well with no lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly. Platelets 12 ×10⁹/L; haemoglobin and white cell count normal; blood film otherwise normal. What is the mechanism?',
 o:['IgG autoantibodies against platelet glycoproteins lead to splenic destruction of platelets','Marrow infiltration crowds out megakaryocytes','Platelets are consumed in widespread intravascular clotting','Deficiency of von Willebrand factor','Heparin-dependent antibodies activate platelets'],a:0,
 e:'Immune thrombocytopenia: autoantibodies (often against GPIIb/IIIa or GPIb) coat platelets, which are cleared by splenic macrophages via Fc receptors (type II hypersensitivity). In children it typically follows a viral infection and usually resolves within weeks to months. Abnormal Hb or white cells, bone pain or organomegaly would suggest leukaemia instead.'},
{t:'haem',type:'saq',q:'Which vitamin K-dependent clotting factor has the shortest half-life, explaining why the PT/INR is the first test to rise after starting warfarin or in acute liver failure?',acc:['vii|7'],no:['viii','8','xvii','17'],m:'Factor VII (half-life about 4–6 hours)',
 e:'Factor VII falls within hours, so the PT (extrinsic pathway) lengthens first. Prothrombin (factor II) has a half-life of about 3 days, which is why full anticoagulation with warfarin takes several days even when the INR is already raised.'},
{t:'haem',type:'saq',q:'Thrombin bound to thrombomodulin on healthy endothelium activates a vitamin K-dependent protein that, with protein S, cleaves factors Va and VIIIa. Name it.',acc:['proteinc|apc'],no:['proteins'],m:'Protein C (activated protein C)',
 e:'This is negative feedback: thrombin made at the wound edge, once it reaches intact endothelium, switches from making fibrin to activating protein C, which switches off tenase and prothrombinase. Factor V Leiden makes Va resistant to activated protein C, causing thrombophilia.'},
{t:'haem',type:'saq',q:'A 79-year-old taking dabigatran has an intracranial haemorrhage. Name the specific reversal agent.',acc:['idarucizumab|praxbind'],m:'Idarucizumab',
 e:'Idarucizumab is a humanised monoclonal antibody fragment (Fab) that binds dabigatran with very high affinity, neutralising it within minutes. Andexanet alfa (a decoy factor Xa) reverses apixaban and rivaroxaban. Neither works against warfarin, which needs vitamin K and prothrombin complex concentrate.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'Aspirin is very effective at preventing myocardial infarction but much less effective than anticoagulants at preventing DVT. What is the best explanation?',
 o:['Venous clots form in slow flow and are rich in fibrin and red cells, whereas arterial clots form on ruptured plaque under high shear and are platelet-rich','Aspirin cannot reach the venous circulation','Veins contain no platelets','Aspirin activates clotting factors in veins','Venous clots form by a completely platelet-independent process that aspirin enhances'],a:0,
 e:'Arterial thrombi ("white clots") depend on platelet adhesion and aggregation at high shear on damaged plaque, so antiplatelets work best. Venous thrombi ("red clots") form where flow is slow (stasis, Virchow), dominated by coagulation and fibrin trapping red cells, so anticoagulants (LMWH, DOACs, warfarin) are used for DVT and PE.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'In the cell-based model of coagulation, people with haemophilia have a normal tissue factor–VIIa pathway, yet they bleed. Why?',
 o:['TFPI quickly shuts down TF–VIIa–Xa, so the large thrombin burst depends on the IXa–VIIIa (tenase) complex on activated platelets','Factor VIII is needed for platelets to adhere to collagen','Factor IX converts fibrinogen to fibrin','The extrinsic pathway does not exist in the body','Factor VIII is needed for vascular spasm'],a:0,
 e:'Initiation by TF–VIIa makes only a trace of thrombin before TFPI switches it off. Propagation needs tenase (IXa + VIIIa) assembled on activated platelets, which makes Xa about 50 times faster and drives the thrombin burst. Without VIII or IX, a soft platelet plug forms but is not stabilised, so bleeding is delayed and deep.'},
{t:'haem',type:'sba',q:'A 70-year-old presents 90 minutes after the onset of a left middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke. CT excludes haemorrhage and IV alteplase is considered. What does alteplase do, and what is its main risk?',
 o:['Converts plasminogen to plasmin; intracranial haemorrhage','Inhibits thrombin; heparin-induced thrombocytopenia','Blocks platelet P2Y₁₂; gastrointestinal bleeding','Inhibits factor Xa; hypocalcaemia','Breaks down fibrinogen directly; anaphylaxis is the main risk'],a:0,
 e:'Alteplase is recombinant tissue plasminogen activator. It converts fibrin-bound plasminogen to plasmin, which digests fibrin and reopens the artery. The benefit is time-critical (within 4.5 hours of onset). The main risk is symptomatic intracranial haemorrhage, so it is contraindicated with recent bleeding, surgery or very high blood pressure.'},
/* ---------- wound healing ---------- */
{t:'wound',type:'sba',q:'In classic experiments, wounds in animals depleted of one cell type failed to form granulation tissue and healed very poorly, even without infection. Depleting neutrophils did not have this effect. Which cell had been depleted?',
 o:['Macrophages','Mast cells','Eosinophils','NK cells','Basophils'],a:0,
 e:'Macrophages are the conductors of repair. After cleaning up, they switch to a repair (M2) phenotype and release PDGF, TGF-β, VEGF and FGF to recruit fibroblasts and drive angiogenesis. Neutrophils mainly kill bacteria; a clean wound can heal without them. This is one reason steroids (which suppress macrophages) delay healing.'},
{t:'wound',type:'sba',q:'A 63-year-old with long-standing type 2 diabetes has a painless, deep ulcer under the first metatarsal head with thick callus around it. Foot pulses are present and the foot is warm. What is the main reason the ulcer formed?',
 o:['Loss of protective sensation, so repetitive pressure and minor injury go unnoticed','Arterial occlusion causing tissue ischaemia','Venous hypertension','Vitamin C deficiency','A keloid reaction'],a:0,
 e:'Neuropathic ulcers form at pressure points because the patient cannot feel damage. Diabetes also impairs healing by microvascular disease, poor neutrophil function and hyperglycaemia. Management: off-loading pressure, debridement of callus, infection control and urgent multidisciplinary foot care. Pulses and warmth make a primarily arterial ulcer less likely.'},
{t:'wound',type:'sba',q:'A 74-year-old smoker has a painful, punched-out ulcer on the tip of his toe. The foot is cold and pale with absent pulses, and he hangs his leg out of bed at night to ease the pain. Which treatment must be avoided until his circulation has been assessed?',
 o:['High compression bandaging','Analgesia','Referral to the vascular team','Stopping smoking','Simple non-adherent dressings'],a:0,
 e:'This is an arterial (ischaemic) ulcer: rest pain relieved by dependency, punched-out edges, distal location. Compression, the mainstay for venous ulcers, can cause further ischaemia and necrosis in arterial disease. The ankle–brachial pressure index (ABPI) is checked before any compression; an ABPI below about 0.8 contraindicates high compression.'},
{t:'wound',type:'saq',q:'In which phase of wound healing does granulation tissue fill the defect?',acc:['prolif'],m:'The proliferative phase',
 e:'From about day 3 to week 3, angiogenesis and fibroblast proliferation create granulation tissue: new capillary loops, fibroblasts, macrophages and a loose matrix of fibronectin, hyaluronic acid and type III collagen. Epithelialisation and contraction also happen in this phase.'},
{t:'wound',type:'saq',q:'Lysyl oxidase cross-links collagen during remodelling and gives scars their tensile strength. Which trace element does it need?',acc:['copper'],m:'Copper',
 e:'Lysyl oxidase is copper-dependent. Copper deficiency (and Menkes disease, a defect of copper transport) causes weak connective tissue. Zinc is needed for matrix metalloproteinases and cell division; vitamin C for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylation.'},
{t:'wound',type:'sba',q:'On day 10, a pressure ulcer has a bright red, granular, moist base that bleeds easily when touched. What is this tissue mainly made of?',
 o:['New capillary loops, fibroblasts and macrophages in a loose matrix with type III collagen','Dense, aligned type I collagen with few cells','Dead neutrophils, bacteria and liquefied tissue','Necrotic slough','Regenerated keratinocytes'],a:0,
 e:'Healthy granulation tissue looks red and granular because of the many new capillary loops. It bleeds easily because those vessels are fragile. Pale or grey granulation suggests poor blood supply or infection; overgrowth above the skin surface ("hypergranulation") prevents epithelium closing over. Dense type I collagen is the late remodelled scar.'},
/* ---------- inflammation ---------- */
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'What mainly causes the pain of acute inflammation?',
 o:['Bradykinin and prostaglandin E₂ sensitising and activating nociceptors, plus tissue stretch from swelling','Histamine acting alone on pain fibres','C5a binding to nerve endings','Neutrophil elastase digesting nerves','IL-10 released by macrophages'],a:0,
 e:'Bradykinin directly activates nociceptors and PGE₂ lowers their threshold (hyperalgesia); this is why NSAIDs relieve inflammatory pain. Swelling stretches tissue. Histamine mainly causes itch and vasodilation. IL-10 is anti-inflammatory.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'A 70-year-old started on prednisolone 40 mg daily for giant cell arteritis has a white cell count of 15 ×10⁹/L (neutrophils 13) a week later, with no sign of infection. What explains the neutrophilia?',
 o:['Steroids reduce neutrophil adhesion and migration, so cells marginated along vessel walls enter the circulating pool','Steroids cause an occult bacterial infection','Steroids trigger leukaemia','Steroids increase lymphocyte production','Steroids destroy neutrophils in the spleen less effectively'],a:0,
 e:'Corticosteroids reduce expression of adhesion molecules, so neutrophils demarginate and are slower to leave the blood; they also speed release from the marrow. The count rises while lymphocytes and eosinophils fall. This is expected and does not by itself indicate infection, although steroids can mask fever and CRP rises.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'A 45-year-old with viral pericarditis has a scratchy friction rub. At post-mortem in a similar case the pericardial surfaces look like "bread and butter" pulled apart. Which pattern of acute inflammation is this?',
 o:['Fibrinous','Serous','Purulent','Granulomatous','Transudative'],a:0,
 e:'With larger vascular gaps, fibrinogen leaks out and is converted to fibrin, which coats serosal surfaces. The rough surfaces rub together (friction rub). Fibrinous exudate may resolve by fibrinolysis or organise into adhesions (constrictive pericarditis). Purulent exudate (pus) is typical of pyogenic bacteria.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'A gastric biopsy from a patient with years of untreated Helicobacter pylori infection shows inflammation in the lamina propria. Which cells would you expect to predominate?',
 o:['Lymphocytes, plasma cells and macrophages','Neutrophils only','Eosinophils only','Mast cells','Epithelioid granulomas with caseation'],a:0,
 e:'Chronic inflammation is dominated by mononuclear cells: lymphocytes, plasma cells and macrophages, with ongoing tissue destruction and repair. Neutrophils appear in active phases. Chronic H. pylori gastritis can lead to peptic ulcers, atrophy, intestinal metaplasia, gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma (from chronic B cell stimulation).'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'Which process actively switches off acute inflammation and returns tissue to normal?',
 o:['Macrophages eat apoptotic neutrophils (efferocytosis) and lipid mediators such as lipoxins and resolvins stop further recruitment','Neutrophils return from the tissue into the blood','Mast cells reabsorb their histamine','Complement proteins simply run out','Fever kills the inflammatory cells'],a:0,
 e:'Resolution is an active programme. Neutrophils die by apoptosis, keeping their toxic contents inside; macrophages that eat them switch to an anti-inflammatory, repair phenotype (IL-10, TGF-β), and arachidonic acid metabolism shifts from leukotrienes to pro-resolving lipoxins. Failure of resolution leads to chronic inflammation.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'Why are COX-2 selective NSAIDs (such as rofecoxib, later withdrawn) associated with an increased risk of heart attack and stroke?',
 o:['They reduce endothelial prostacyclin while leaving platelet thromboxane A₂ (made by COX-1) untouched','They irreversibly inhibit platelet COX-1','They directly activate clotting factors','They raise LDL cholesterol','They block nitric oxide synthase'],a:0,
 e:'Endothelial PGI₂ (largely COX-2-derived) inhibits platelets and dilates vessels; platelet TXA₂ (COX-1) does the opposite. Selective COX-2 inhibition tips the balance towards thrombosis. NSAIDs also raise blood pressure and cause fluid retention. Celecoxib remains in use with cardiovascular cautions; it does protect the stomach more than non-selective NSAIDs.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'saq',q:'Which leukotriene is the main lipid chemoattractant for neutrophils?',acc:['ltb|leukotrieneb'],no:['ltc','ltd','lte'],m:'Leukotriene B₄ (LTB₄)',
 e:'LTB₄, made via 5-lipoxygenase, attracts and activates neutrophils. The cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC₄, LTD₄, LTE₄) cause bronchoconstriction and leakage instead and are blocked at their receptor by montelukast. Other neutrophil chemoattractants are IL-8 (CXCL8), C5a and bacterial formyl peptides.'},
{t:'inflam',type:'sba',q:'A 30-year-old man has a 4 cm tender, fluctuant swelling in his axilla with a pointing yellow centre. Why is incision and drainage needed rather than antibiotics alone?',
 o:['Antibiotics penetrate poorly into an avascular cavity of pus, where bacteria grow slowly and the low pH reduces drug activity','Abscesses are usually caused by viruses','Antibiotics cannot kill S. aureus','Drainage is needed only to take a sample for culture','Pus contains antibiotic-destroying enzymes from neutrophils in every case'],a:0,
 e:'An abscess is a walled-off collection of dead neutrophils, bacteria and liquefied tissue surrounded by fibrin and granulation tissue. It has no blood supply inside, so drugs reach it poorly, and the acidic, protein-rich pus and dormant bacteria blunt antibiotic action. Drainage removes the bulk of organisms; antibiotics are added for surrounding cellulitis or systemic illness.'},
/* ---------- innate ---------- */
{t:'innate',type:'saq',q:'A 30-year-old man with infertility (immotile sperm) has had recurrent sinusitis since childhood, bronchiectasis and dextrocardia. Name the condition.',acc:['ciliary|kartagener'],m:'Primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome when with situs inversus)',
 e:'Dynein arm defects stop cilia beating, so the mucociliary escalator fails: mucus and microbes pool in sinuses, middle ears and bronchi, causing chronic infection and bronchiectasis. Sperm tails share the same structure, and embryonic nodal cilia set left–right orientation, explaining situs inversus in about half of patients.'},
{t:'innate',type:'sba',q:'Lysozyme in tears and saliva is an innate antibacterial defence. How does it work?',
 o:['It hydrolyses bonds in peptidoglycan, so it is most effective against Gram-positive bacteria','It forms pores in bacterial membranes like the MAC','It binds iron so bacteria cannot grow','It opsonises bacteria for phagocytosis','It blocks the bacterial 30S ribosome'],a:0,
 e:'Lysozyme cleaves β-1,4 links between N-acetylmuramic acid and N-acetylglucosamine in peptidoglycan. Gram-positives expose thick peptidoglycan; in Gram-negatives the outer membrane protects it. Lactoferrin is the iron-binding protein; defensins form membrane pores.'},
{t:'innate',type:'sba',q:'Which of these is a damage-associated molecular pattern (DAMP) rather than a pathogen-associated pattern (PAMP)?',
 o:['Monosodium urate crystals','Lipopolysaccharide','Flagellin','Unmethylated CpG DNA','Double-stranded viral RNA'],a:0,
 e:'DAMPs are host molecules released or exposed by damaged or dying cells: urate crystals, ATP, HMGB1, heat shock proteins, DNA outside the nucleus. They trigger sterile inflammation through the same PRRs (here the NLRP3 inflammasome, causing gout). The other options are microbial PAMPs.'},
{t:'innate',type:'sba',q:'In the first days of a viral infection, before CD8 T cells are ready, which cytokine from macrophages and dendritic cells most strongly activates NK cells (alongside type I interferons)?',
 o:['IL-12','IL-4','IL-10','TGF-β','IL-5'],a:0,
 e:'IL-12 (with IL-15, IL-18 and type I IFNs) activates NK cells to kill and to secrete IFN-γ, which in turn activates macrophages and pushes CD4 cells towards Th1. IL-4 and IL-5 are Th2 cytokines; IL-10 and TGF-β are anti-inflammatory.'},
{t:'innate',type:'saq',q:'Monosodium urate crystals in gout are sensed by a cytoplasmic multiprotein complex that activates caspase-1 to produce mature IL-1β. What is this complex called?',acc:['inflammasome|nlrp3'],m:'The NLRP3 inflammasome',
 e:'NLRP3 assembles with ASC and pro-caspase-1. Caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1β and pro-IL-18 into their active forms. This explains why IL-1 blockade (anakinra, canakinumab) is effective in gout and in autoinflammatory diseases caused by NLRP3 mutations.'},
{t:'innate',type:'sba',q:'In a patient with severe inflammation, which plasma protein falls (a "negative" acute phase protein)?',
 o:['Albumin','C-reactive protein','Fibrinogen','Ferritin','Serum amyloid A'],a:0,
 e:'IL-6 redirects hepatic synthesis towards acute phase proteins (CRP, fibrinogen, ferritin, serum amyloid A, haptoglobin, complement C3), while albumin and transferrin production falls. Low albumin in acutely unwell patients therefore often reflects inflammation rather than malnutrition. Ferritin rises in inflammation, which can mask iron deficiency.'},
{t:'innate',type:'sba',q:'During a rapid IV vancomycin infusion, a patient becomes flushed and itchy over the face, neck and upper body, with mild hypotension. It settles when the infusion is slowed. What is the mechanism?',
 o:['Direct, non-IgE-mediated release of histamine from mast cells and basophils','IgE-mediated anaphylaxis','Immune complex deposition','A delayed T cell reaction','Endotoxin release from killed bacteria'],a:0,
 e:'"Red man" (vancomycin flushing) syndrome is an infusion-rate-related anaphylactoid reaction: vancomycin activates mast cells directly (via MRGPRX2), without prior sensitisation or IgE. Slowing the infusion and giving antihistamines prevents it; it is not a true allergy and does not prevent future use. Opioids and contrast media can act similarly.'},
/* ---------- complement ---------- */
{t:'compl',type:'sba',q:'A 3-year-old has recurrent severe pneumococcal and Haemophilus influenzae infections. Both CH50 and AP50 are very low and C3 is undetectable. Which complement function, lost here, best explains this pattern of infection?',
 o:['Opsonisation of bacteria by C3b','Inhibition of C1 by C1-inhibitor','Formation of the membrane attack complex alone','Mast cell activation by C5a alone','Protection of host cells by CD59'],a:0,
 e:'C3 is the hub of all three pathways. Without C3b, encapsulated bacteria cannot be opsonised for phagocytes and are cleared poorly, so the picture resembles antibody deficiency. Pure MAC (C5–C9) defects instead cause Neisseria infections. C3 deficiency also impairs immune complex clearance.'},
{t:'compl',type:'sba',q:'Deficiency of early classical pathway components (C1q, C2 or C4) carries a strong risk of systemic lupus erythematosus. Why?',
 o:['Immune complexes and apoptotic cells are cleared poorly, so self antigens persist and drive autoimmunity','These proteins normally kill autoreactive B cells','The membrane attack complex cannot form','Hereditary angioedema develops','C3 is overproduced'],a:0,
 e:'C1q and C4b coat apoptotic cells and immune complexes so they are removed quietly by phagocytes and red cell CR1. Without them, nuclear antigens accumulate and break tolerance. C1q deficiency carries the highest SLE risk (over 90%); C2 deficiency is the commonest.'},
{t:'compl',type:'sba',q:'Which complement pathway is triggered by a liver-made pattern-recognition protein binding sugar arrays on microbes, and therefore works before any antibody has been made?',
 o:['The lectin pathway','The classical pathway','The terminal pathway','The kinin pathway','The fibrinolytic pathway'],a:0,
 e:'Mannose-binding lectin (an acute phase protein) and ficolins bind microbial mannose and N-acetylglucosamine arrays and activate MASP-2, which cleaves C4 and C2 just like C1s. The alternative pathway also acts without antibody but is triggered by spontaneous C3 tickover rather than a recognition molecule. The classical pathway usually needs antibody.'},
{t:'compl',type:'saq',q:'A 6-year-old has microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, low platelets and acute kidney injury without preceding diarrhoea. A mutation is found in the main plasma regulator of the alternative pathway. Name this regulator.',acc:['factorh|cfh'],m:'Factor H',
 e:'Atypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome: loss of factor H (or factor I, MCP) lets the alternative pathway run unchecked on glomerular endothelium, causing thrombotic microangiopathy. It is treated with C5 blockade (eculizumab). Typical HUS follows Shiga toxin-producing E. coli diarrhoea.'},
{t:'compl',type:'saq',q:'Name the C3 convertase of the alternative pathway.',acc:['c3bbb'],m:'C3bBb (stabilised by properdin)',
 e:'C3b binds factor B, which factor D cleaves, leaving C3bBb. Properdin stabilises it on microbial surfaces. Adding another C3b makes the C5 convertase C3bBb3b. The classical and lectin C3 convertase is C4b2a.'},
{t:'compl',type:'sba',q:'Which complement fragments trigger mast cell degranulation (the anaphylatoxins)?',
 o:['C3a and C5a','C3b and C4b','C5b–9','C1q','Factor B and factor D'],a:0,
 e:'C3a and C5a (and weakly C4a) bind receptors on mast cells and basophils, releasing histamine: vasodilation and leaky vessels. C5a is the most potent and is also chemotactic for neutrophils. C3b and C4b are opsonins; C5b–9 is the membrane attack complex.'},
/* ---------- lymphatics ---------- */
{t:'lymph',type:'sba',q:'A 72-year-old with right-sided heart failure has bilateral pitting ankle oedema. Which Starling mechanism mainly explains it?',
 o:['Raised venous and capillary hydrostatic pressure increases filtration beyond what lymphatics can return','Reduced plasma oncotic pressure from protein loss','Lymphatic obstruction by tumour','Increased capillary permeability from histamine','Reduced interstitial hydrostatic pressure'],a:0,
 e:'High central venous pressure is transmitted back to capillaries, so more fluid is filtered than reabsorbed. Lymphatics can increase their flow several-fold, but once overwhelmed fluid accumulates, most in dependent areas. Because the fluid is protein-poor it pits easily. Low albumin (nephrotic syndrome, liver failure) causes oedema by the oncotic mechanism instead.'},
{t:'lymph',type:'sba',q:'A 66-year-old man with weight loss and early satiety has a hard, painless node in the left supraclavicular fossa (Virchow\'s node). Where is the primary cancer most likely to be?',
 o:['The stomach or another abdominal organ','The right breast','The scalp','The right hand','The left lower leg'],a:0,
 e:'Lymph from the abdomen drains via the cisterna chyli and thoracic duct into the left venous angle, so abdominal cancers (classically gastric) can spread to the left supraclavicular node (Troisier\'s sign). Malignant nodes are hard, painless and fixed; reactive nodes are soft and tender.'},
{t:'lymph',type:'saq',q:'A man from rural India has massive swelling and thickened skin of both legs after years of recurrent fever and lymphangitis. The nematode responsible is spread by mosquitoes and lives in the lymphatics. Name it.',acc:['wuchereria|brugia|filari'],m:'Wuchereria bancrofti (lymphatic filariasis)',
 e:'Adult worms block and damage lymphatic vessels, causing lymphoedema and, with recurrent bacterial infections and fibrosis, elephantiasis. Microfilariae circulate at night. Treatment includes diethylcarbamazine or ivermectin with albendazole; mass drug administration is used for elimination.'},
{t:'lymph',type:'saq',q:'What are the lymphatic capillaries in the intestinal villi that absorb dietary fat as chylomicrons called?',acc:['lacteal'],m:'Lacteals',
 e:'Long-chain fats are packaged into chylomicrons by enterocytes and are too large to enter blood capillaries, so they enter lacteals. The milky lymph (chyle) travels via the cisterna chyli and thoracic duct to the left venous angle.'},
/* ---------- adaptive ---------- */
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'Cytotoxic T cells from a person who is HLA-A2 kill cells from their own body infected with influenza, but not influenza-infected cells from an HLA-A1 person who shares no class I alleles with them. Why?',
 o:['T cells recognise peptide only in the context of their own MHC molecules (MHC restriction)','HLA-A1 cells cannot be infected by influenza','Influenza peptides are presented only on MHC II','The HLA-A1 cells have more NK inhibitory receptors','CD8 T cells recognise whole viral proteins, not peptides'],a:0,
 e:'During positive selection in the thymic cortex, T cells are selected for TCRs that can bind self-MHC. A TCR recognises a combined surface of peptide plus a particular MHC molecule. This is MHC restriction (Zinkernagel and Doherty).'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'In the thymic cortex, what happens to developing T cells whose receptors cannot bind self-MHC at all?',
 o:['They die by neglect, failing positive selection','They are deleted for binding too strongly (negative selection)','They become regulatory T cells','They become B cells','They leave the thymus as naive T cells'],a:0,
 e:'Positive selection in the cortex rescues only thymocytes whose TCR binds self-MHC with low to moderate affinity; the rest die by neglect. Negative selection in the medulla then deletes those binding self-peptide–MHC too strongly (helped by AIRE). Some moderately self-reactive CD4 cells become FOXP3+ Tregs. Over 95% of thymocytes die.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'In tuberculoid leprosy patients have few, well-defined skin lesions with granulomas and very few bacilli; in lepromatous leprosy there are widespread lesions teeming with bacilli. What best explains the difference?',
 o:['A strong Th1 (IFN-γ, macrophage-activating) response in tuberculoid disease, versus a Th2-skewed, weak cell-mediated response in lepromatous disease','More antibody production in tuberculoid disease','A different species of Mycobacterium causes each form','Neutropenia in lepromatous disease','Complement deficiency in tuberculoid disease'],a:0,
 e:'Mycobacterium leprae lives in macrophages and Schwann cells. A Th1 response (IL-12, IFN-γ) activates macrophages and forms granulomas that contain the infection. In lepromatous leprosy the response is Th2-skewed with high antibody but poor cell-mediated immunity, so bacteria multiply freely. It is a classic demonstration that antibodies do not control intracellular bacteria.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'A patient with metastatic melanoma is treated with ipilimumab, an antibody that blocks CTLA-4. Six weeks later he develops severe diarrhoea from colitis. What links the treatment and the side effect?',
 o:['CTLA-4 normally competes with CD28 for CD80/86 and brakes T cell activation; blocking it boosts anti-tumour and self-reactive T cells','Ipilimumab is directly toxic to colonic epithelium','Ipilimumab depletes B cells','Ipilimumab blocks IL-2 production','Ipilimumab activates complement on gut bacteria'],a:0,
 e:'CTLA-4 binds CD80/86 with higher affinity than CD28 and removes co-stimulation, damping T cell priming; Tregs use it to suppress. Checkpoint blockade (CTLA-4, PD-1, PD-L1) releases the brakes on T cells against the tumour but also against self, causing immune-related adverse events: colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis, hypophysitis, pneumonitis. Treated with steroids.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'Why is the immune response to a second exposure to the same pathogen faster and stronger than the first?',
 o:['Many more antigen-specific memory cells exist, with a lower activation threshold, and memory B cells are already class-switched and affinity-matured','Innate immunity remembers the pathogen through PRRs','Antibodies from the first response last for life at the same level','The pathogen is weaker the second time','Memory T cells no longer need antigen to be presented'],a:0,
 e:'After the primary response contracts, a population of memory cells remains that is 10 to 1000 times larger than the original naive pool for that antigen. Memory cells are activated more easily and home to tissues, and memory B cells make high-affinity IgG straight away. This is the basis of vaccination.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'saq',q:'Which genes encode the enzymes that cut DNA at recombination signal sequences to join V, D and J segments, and whose loss causes SCID with no T or B cells?',acc:['rag'],m:'RAG1 and RAG2',
 e:'RAG1 and RAG2 make double-strand breaks at recombination signal sequences; the DNA is then repaired by non-homologous end joining (Artemis, DNA-PK). Without them no TCR or BCR can be made, so no T or B cells develop (T−B−NK+ SCID). Partial defects cause Omenn syndrome.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'A woman receives a kidney from her HLA-identical sister but still needs lifelong immunosuppression. Why?',
 o:['Minor histocompatibility antigens (peptides from other polymorphic proteins) differ and can still be recognised by T cells','HLA-identical siblings have different ABO groups in every case','Immunosuppression is needed only to prevent infection','The kidney carries the donor\'s B cells, which reject the recipient','Identical HLA means T cells cannot recognise the graft at all'],a:0,
 e:'Apart from identical twins, siblings differ at many other genes. Polymorphic proteins produce "minor" antigens that are presented on shared HLA and recognised by recipient T cells. Rejection is milder than with HLA-mismatched grafts, but immunosuppression is still required.'},
{t:'adapt',type:'sba',q:'Cytotoxic T cells kill virus-infected cells with very little inflammation. Why?',
 o:['They trigger apoptosis through perforin and granzymes (or Fas ligand), so the cell dies without spilling its contents','They lyse cells with the membrane attack complex','They phagocytose the infected cells','They release histamine onto target cells','They coat cells with antibody for neutrophils'],a:0,
 e:'Granzymes enter through perforin pores and activate caspases, and Fas ligand triggers the extrinsic apoptosis pathway. Apoptotic cells shrink and fragment into membrane-bound bodies that are quietly eaten. Necrosis, by contrast, releases DAMPs and causes inflammation.'},
/* ---------- antibodies ---------- */
{t:'ab',type:'sba',q:'A group O mother has a group A baby. The baby develops mild jaundice but no anaemia. Why is ABO haemolytic disease usually much milder than rhesus disease?',
 o:['Most anti-A and anti-B are IgM, which cannot cross the placenta, and fetal A and B antigens are weakly expressed and also found on other tissues','ABO antibodies are made only after birth','Fetal red cells lack A antigen completely','IgG anti-A is destroyed by the placenta','ABO antibodies are IgE'],a:0,
 e:'"Natural" anti-A and anti-B are mainly IgM. Group O mothers also make some IgG anti-A/B, which can cross the placenta, but A and B antigens on fetal red cells are weakly developed and soak up antibody on other tissues, so haemolysis is mild. Anti-D after sensitisation is IgG, causing severe disease in later pregnancies.'},
{t:'ab',type:'sba',q:'How does dimeric IgA made by plasma cells in the gut lamina propria reach the gut lumen?',
 o:['It binds the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor on the basolateral surface and is carried across the epithelium; part of the receptor stays attached as secretory component','It diffuses through gaps between epithelial cells','It is secreted directly by enterocytes','It is carried in the bile only','It is transported by FcRn'],a:0,
 e:'Transcytosis via the polymeric Ig receptor (pIgR) delivers IgA to the lumen. Cleavage leaves the extracellular part of the receptor bound as secretory component, which protects secretory IgA from gut proteases. FcRn handles IgG.'},
{t:'ab',type:'sba',q:'Before starting rituximab for rheumatoid arthritis, which infection must be screened for because the drug can cause a dangerous reactivation?',
 o:['Hepatitis B','Human papillomavirus','Rotavirus','Measles','Helicobacter pylori'],a:0,
 e:'Rituximab depletes CD20+ B cells, removing antibody-mediated control of hepatitis B. Reactivation can cause fulminant hepatitis, so HBsAg and anti-HBc are checked, and antiviral prophylaxis (entecavir or tenofovir) is given where needed. Rituximab can also cause low immunoglobulin levels and, rarely, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy.'},
{t:'ab',type:'sba',q:'A baby born to a mother with HIV tests positive for HIV antibodies at 3 months. The baby received antiretroviral prophylaxis after birth. What is the best interpretation?',
 o:['The result may only reflect maternal IgG that crossed the placenta; HIV nucleic acid testing (PCR) is needed','The baby is definitely infected','The baby has made IgM against HIV','The test proves the baby is not infected','The baby has been vaccinated against HIV'],a:0,
 e:'Maternal IgG (including anti-HIV) crosses the placenta and can persist for up to 18–24 months, so antibody tests cannot diagnose infection in infants. HIV DNA or RNA PCR at set intervals is used. The same principle applies to other congenital infections, where infant IgM (which does not cross the placenta) is more informative.'},
{t:'ab',type:'sba',q:'Where does somatic hypermutation of antibody genes take place?',
 o:['In the dark zone of germinal centres in secondary lymphoid follicles','In the thymic cortex','In the bone marrow before antigen exposure','In the paracortex of lymph nodes','In the red pulp of the spleen'],a:0,
 e:'After T cell help, activated B cells proliferate in the germinal centre dark zone, where AID introduces mutations into the variable regions. In the light zone, B cells test their new receptors on antigen held by follicular dendritic cells and are selected by Tfh cells. V(D)J recombination happens earlier, in the bone marrow.'},
{t:'ab',type:'saq',q:'Which antibody class is most abundant in colostrum and breast milk, protecting the baby\'s gut?',acc:['iga'],m:'Secretory IgA',
 e:'Breast milk IgA is specific for microbes the mother has met (via the enteromammary link: B cells primed in the mother\'s gut home to the breast). It coats microbes in the infant\'s gut without triggering inflammation. Transplacental IgG gives systemic protection.'},
{t:'ab',type:'sba',q:'A 79-year-old with severe digoxin toxicity is given digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Fab only, no Fc). Which property allows them to work despite lacking an Fc region?',
 o:['Binding and neutralising the drug requires only the antigen-binding (variable) region','Fc is needed for binding, so they work by complement','They activate NK cells through CD16','They are cleared by FcRn recycling','They opsonise digoxin for phagocytes'],a:0,
 e:'Neutralisation needs only the Fab arms. Fab fragments are small, distribute quickly into tissues, are cleared by the kidney, and without an Fc they do not activate complement or Fc receptors, which reduces adverse reactions. Idarucizumab (for dabigatran) and antivenoms use the same principle.'},
{t:'ab',type:'saq',q:'IgM has low affinity binding sites but ten of them. What term describes the total binding strength of all the sites of a multivalent antibody together?',acc:['avidity'],m:'Avidity',
 e:'Affinity is the strength of one paratope–epitope interaction; avidity is the overall strength of multiple simultaneous bonds. Like velcro, many weak hooks give a strong hold, so IgM binds repeating surface antigens strongly early in the response, before affinity maturation has produced high-affinity IgG.'},
/* ---------- hypersensitivity ---------- */
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'A baby born to a mother with myasthenia gravis has a weak cry, poor sucking and floppiness for the first two weeks, then recovers completely. Why?',
 o:['Maternal IgG against acetylcholine receptors crossed the placenta and was gradually cleared','The baby has inherited congenital myasthenia','Maternal T cells crossed the placenta and attacked the baby\'s muscles','The baby was exposed to the mother\'s pyridostigmine','The baby made its own anti-acetylcholine receptor IgM'],a:0,
 e:'Transient neonatal myasthenia is passive transfer of the mother\'s IgG autoantibody via FcRn. It shows that myasthenia gravis is antibody-mediated (type II, blocking the receptor). Symptoms fade over weeks as maternal IgG is cleared. Neonatal Graves\' disease follows the same logic with TSH receptor-stimulating IgG.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'A 32-year-old woman has weight loss, tremor, heat intolerance, a smooth goitre and bulging eyes. TSH is suppressed and free T4 is high. Which hypersensitivity mechanism is responsible?',
 o:['Type II: IgG stimulates the TSH receptor','Type II: IgG blocks the acetylcholine receptor','Type III: immune complexes deposit in the thyroid','Type IV: T cells destroy thyroid follicles','Type I: IgE triggers thyroid mast cells'],a:0,
 e:'Graves\' disease: TSH receptor antibodies (TRAb) mimic TSH, driving hormone production and thyroid growth regardless of feedback. It is the classic stimulating type II reaction (sometimes called type V). Hashimoto\'s thyroiditis is mainly T cell-mediated destruction with anti-TPO antibodies.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'An adult with allergic asthma wheezes within 10 minutes of exposure to cat dander, improves with salbutamol, then becomes wheezy again 6 hours later. What causes the second episode?',
 o:['The late-phase reaction: recruited eosinophils and Th2 cells release leukotrienes and cytokines','A second dose of histamine stored in mast cells','A type III reaction to cat proteins','Salbutamol rebound','A new IgE response made in 6 hours'],a:0,
 e:'Mast cell degranulation causes the immediate phase (histamine, leukotrienes, PGD₂). Cytokines and chemokines released at the same time recruit eosinophils, basophils and Th2 cells, which drive a late phase 4–12 hours later with airway oedema and hyper-responsiveness. Inhaled corticosteroids blunt the late phase; β₂-agonists mainly relieve the early bronchoconstriction.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'Thirty days after an allogeneic bone marrow transplant, a patient develops a rash on the palms and soles, profuse diarrhoea and jaundice. What is happening?',
 o:['Donor T cells in the graft are attacking the recipient\'s tissues (graft-versus-host disease)','The recipient\'s T cells are rejecting the marrow','Pre-formed antibodies are causing hyperacute rejection','Immune complexes from transfused blood are depositing in the skin','It is a drug-induced type I reaction'],a:0,
 e:'Acute GvHD: immunocompetent donor T cells recognise recipient HLA (and minor antigens) as foreign, attacking skin, gut and liver. Risk rises with HLA mismatch; prevention includes T cell depletion and immunosuppression (e.g. ciclosporin, methotrexate). A controlled graft-versus-leukaemia effect is part of the benefit of transplantation.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'saq',q:'A patient has haemolytic anaemia and spherocytes on the film. A direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is positive. What does this test detect on the patient\'s red cells?',acc:['antibod|igg|immunoglobulin|complement|c3'],m:'Antibody (IgG) and/or complement (C3d) already bound to the patient\'s red cells',
 e:'The direct antiglobulin test adds anti-human-globulin reagent to washed patient red cells: if IgG or C3d is already coating them, the cells agglutinate. A positive test indicates immune haemolysis (autoimmune, haemolytic disease of the newborn, transfusion reactions, some drug reactions). Spherocytes form when splenic macrophages nibble antibody-coated membrane.'},
{t:'hyper',type:'sba',q:'A 24-year-old woman has a photosensitive malar rash, arthritis, mouth ulcers and proteinuria. Anti-dsDNA antibodies are high, and C3 and C4 are low. What causes most of the tissue damage?',
 o:['Deposition of immune complexes in tissues, activating complement and recruiting neutrophils','IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation','Cytotoxic T cells killing kidney cells directly','Bacterial infection of the kidney','Direct antibody stimulation of hormone receptors'],a:0,
 e:'SLE is largely type III: complexes of autoantibody with nuclear antigens (released by poorly cleared apoptotic cells) deposit in glomeruli, skin, joints and serosa, consuming complement. Some features are type II (autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia). Low C3/C4 and rising anti-dsDNA indicate active disease.'},
/* ---------- immunodeficiency and HIV ---------- */
{t:'idef',type:'sba',q:'Which combination does a fourth-generation HIV test detect, shortening the window period?',
 o:['HIV p24 antigen and HIV antibodies','HIV RNA only','CD4 count and viral load','HIV antibodies only','Reverse transcriptase activity'],a:0,
 e:'p24 capsid antigen appears about 2–3 weeks after infection, before antibodies, so combined antigen–antibody tests are positive earlier; in UK guidance a negative fourth-generation test 45 days after the last exposure excludes infection. Positive screens are confirmed with further tests. Viral load (RNA PCR) is used for monitoring and infant diagnosis.'},
{t:'idef',type:'sba',q:'A 38-year-old with untreated HIV (CD4 40 cells/mm³) has headache, fever and new left-sided weakness. MRI shows several ring-enhancing lesions, and Toxoplasma IgG is positive. Which organism is most likely?',
 o:['Toxoplasma gondii','Cryptococcus neoformans','Streptococcus pneumoniae','JC virus','Plasmodium falciparum'],a:0,
 e:'Cerebral toxoplasmosis is reactivation of latent cysts when CD4 is below about 100: multiple ring-enhancing lesions, typically in the basal ganglia. It is treated with pyrimethamine, sulfadiazine and folinic acid; co-trimoxazole prophylaxis prevents it. Primary CNS lymphoma (EBV) is the main differential. Cryptococcus causes meningitis; JC virus causes PML without enhancement.'},
{t:'idef',type:'sba',q:'A 55-year-old who has taken prednisolone 30 mg daily for 4 months develops Pneumocystis pneumonia. Which arm of immunity is mainly impaired?',
 o:['T cell-mediated immunity','Complement','Antibody production alone','Neutrophil numbers','Mucosal barriers'],a:0,
 e:'Glucocorticoids cause lymphopenia (especially CD4), suppress cytokine production (IL-2, IL-12, IFN-γ) and macrophage activation. This exposes patients to opportunistic infections normally controlled by T cells, such as Pneumocystis, so prophylaxis is considered in prolonged high-dose steroid use, especially with other immunosuppressants. Neutrophil counts typically rise.'},
{t:'idef',type:'saq',q:'Name an HIV integrase inhibitor commonly used in first-line antiretroviral therapy.',acc:['tegravir'],m:'Dolutegravir (or bictegravir, raltegravir, cabotegravir)',
 e:'Integrase strand transfer inhibitors stop viral DNA being inserted into the host genome. Dolutegravir and bictegravir have a high barrier to resistance and are first-line partners for two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors. Long-acting injectable cabotegravir is used for treatment and PrEP.'},
{t:'idef',type:'sba',q:'A man has taken antiretroviral therapy for a year and his HIV viral load has been undetectable (below 200 copies/mL) for more than 6 months. What is the risk of him passing HIV to a sexual partner?',
 o:['Effectively zero: undetectable equals untransmittable','About 50% per year','The same as before treatment','Only reduced if he also takes PrEP','Reduced by half'],a:0,
 e:'Large studies (PARTNER, HPTN 052) found no linked sexual transmissions from people with sustained viral suppression. This "U=U" message reduces stigma and is the basis of "treatment as prevention". Condoms still protect against other STIs and pregnancy.'},
/* ---------- vaccines ---------- */
{t:'vacc',type:'sba',q:'Why do subunit and recombinant protein vaccines need an adjuvant, while live attenuated vaccines usually do not?',
 o:['Purified proteins lack PAMPs, so adjuvants activate innate PRRs to make dendritic cells express co-stimulatory molecules and cytokines','Adjuvants are preservatives that stop bacterial growth in the vial','Adjuvants make the protein cross the placenta','Adjuvants are needed only to reduce injection pain','Adjuvants inactivate any live virus in the vaccine'],a:0,
 e:'Antigen alone gives signal 1 without signal 2 and can cause tolerance. Live vaccines contain PAMPs and replicate, giving a strong innate signal. Adjuvants (aluminium salts, AS01 in Shingrix, oil-in-water emulsions) create a depot and inflammation, or directly trigger TLRs, so dendritic cells mature and prime T cells.'},
{t:'vacc',type:'sba',q:'Which of these vaccines in the UK childhood schedule is live attenuated?',
 o:['Rotavirus vaccine','6-in-1 vaccine','MenB vaccine','Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine','HPV vaccine'],a:0,
 e:'Oral rotavirus vaccine (8 and 12 weeks) is live attenuated, as are MMRV, nasal flu and BCG. It is avoided in severe immunodeficiency (e.g. SCID), and there is a small risk of intussusception, so it is not started after 15 weeks. The others are inactivated, subunit, recombinant or conjugate vaccines.'},
{t:'vacc',type:'sba',q:'Pertussis vaccine is offered to pregnant women from 16 weeks (ideally between 20 and 32 weeks). How does it mainly protect the newborn?',
 o:['Maternal IgG against pertussis crosses the placenta and protects the baby until its own vaccines at 8 weeks','The vaccine crosses the placenta and immunises the fetus','Breast milk IgA is the only protection','It prevents the mother passing on IgE','The baby\'s T cells are primed in the womb'],a:0,
 e:'Whooping cough is most dangerous in the first months of life, before the 6-in-1 vaccine at 8, 12 and 16 weeks. Vaccinating in pregnancy boosts maternal IgG, which is actively transported via FcRn, mainly in the third trimester. Flu and RSV vaccines are also offered in pregnancy for the same reason.'},
{t:'vacc',type:'saq',q:'A man bitten by a bat is given rabies immunoglobulin into the wound alongside a course of rabies vaccine. What type of immunity does the immunoglobulin give?',acc:['passive'],m:'Artificial passive immunity',
 e:'Pre-formed antibodies give immediate protection while the vaccine induces active immunity, which takes 1–2 weeks. Passive immunity lasts weeks and leaves no memory. Rabies is almost always fatal once symptoms start, so post-exposure prophylaxis is urgent.'},
/* ---------- microbes ---------- */
{t:'micro',type:'sba',q:'Which step of the Gram stain determines whether a bacterium ends up Gram-positive or Gram-negative?',
 o:['Decolourisation with alcohol or acetone','Heat fixing','Crystal violet staining','Adding Gram\'s iodine','Counterstaining with safranin'],a:0,
 e:'All cells take up crystal violet–iodine complexes. Alcohol dissolves the Gram-negative outer membrane and the thin peptidoglycan cannot hold the complex, so the cell loses its colour and takes up the pink safranin. Thick Gram-positive peptidoglycan dehydrates and traps the complex. Over-decolourising can make Gram-positives look negative.'},
{t:'micro',type:'sba',q:'A traveller has profuse "rice-water" diarrhoea of up to 20 litres a day but little fever and no blood. How does the toxin of Vibrio cholerae cause this?',
 o:['It ADP-ribosylates Gs in enterocytes, raising cAMP and opening CFTR chloride channels','It destroys the intestinal villi','It invades the mucosa and causes ulcers','It blocks acetylcholine release in the gut','It acts as a superantigen'],a:0,
 e:'Cholera toxin (an A–B toxin) locks the Gs protein "on". Adenylyl cyclase keeps making cAMP, protein kinase A opens CFTR, and chloride, sodium and water pour into the lumen. The mucosa is intact, so there is no blood or pus. Oral rehydration solution works because the sodium–glucose cotransporter (SGLT1) still functions.'},
{t:'micro',type:'sba',q:'A pregnant woman who ate unpasteurised soft cheese develops fever. Blood cultures grow Gram-positive bacilli. Which feature of Listeria monocytogenes helps it spread from cell to cell without meeting antibodies?',
 o:['It escapes the phagosome with listeriolysin O and uses host actin "comet tails" to push into neighbouring cells','It produces a thick polysaccharide capsule','It forms spores inside cells','It secretes IgA protease','It has an outer membrane rich in LPS'],a:0,
 e:'Listeria lives inside cells: it breaks out of the phagosome, multiplies in the cytoplasm and polymerises host actin to move directly into adjacent cells, hiding from antibody and complement. Control depends on T cells (Th1, CD8). Pregnancy, neonates, old age and immunosuppression raise risk; it can cause miscarriage and neonatal meningitis. Amoxicillin (often with gentamicin) is used, as cephalosporins do not work.'},
{t:'micro',type:'sba',q:'A 72-year-old has a painful, blistering rash in a band across one side of her chest. She had chickenpox as a child. Where has the virus been latent?',
 o:['Sensory (dorsal root or cranial nerve) ganglia','The skin of the chest','Peripheral blood B cells','The spinal cord motor neurons','The liver'],a:0,
 e:'Varicella zoster virus travels up sensory nerves during chickenpox and becomes latent in dorsal root and cranial nerve ganglia. When cell-mediated immunity wanes (age, immunosuppression), it reactivates and travels down the nerve, causing shingles in that dermatome. Antivirals within 72 hours reduce acute symptoms; the Shingrix vaccine prevents it.'},
{t:'micro',type:'saq',q:'Which selective agar is used to grow Gram-negative enteric bacteria from stool or urine, showing whether they ferment lactose?',acc:['macconkey'],m:'MacConkey agar',
 e:'Bile salts and crystal violet inhibit Gram-positives; lactose fermenters (E. coli, Klebsiella) produce acid and form pink colonies, while non-fermenters (Salmonella, Shigella, Pseudomonas) are pale. Blood agar is non-selective and is used for normally sterile sites and to show haemolysis.'},
{t:'micro',type:'sba',q:'Why are most antibacterial drugs ineffective against Candida albicans?',
 o:['Candida is a eukaryote: it has no peptidoglycan wall and uses 80S ribosomes','Candida is a virus','Candida lives only inside cells','Candida is an acid-fast bacterium','Candida has an outer membrane like Gram-negatives'],a:0,
 e:'Fungi share many features with human cells (nucleus, 80S ribosomes, sterol membranes), so antibacterial targets do not exist. Antifungals instead target ergosterol, its synthesis, or the chitin–glucan wall. In fact, broad-spectrum antibiotics encourage thrush by killing competing bacterial flora.'},
/* ---------- pathogens and the response ---------- */
{t:'path',type:'sba',q:'A 26-year-old returning from swimming in Lake Malawi has fever, an itchy rash, cough, and an eosinophil count of 3.2 ×10⁹/L, four weeks after the trip. Which immune response is driving the eosinophilia?',
 o:['A Th2 response with IL-5 and IgE, against invading schistosome larvae and worms','A Th1 response with IFN-γ against intracellular bacteria','A Th17 response against fungi','A neutrophil response to a bacterial abscess','A type III reaction to a virus'],a:0,
 e:'Acute schistosomiasis (Katayama fever) occurs weeks after freshwater exposure as larvae mature. Helminths are too large to phagocytose, so Th2 cells release IL-4 and IL-13 (IgE class switching) and IL-5 (eosinophil production and activation); eosinophils attack antibody-coated worms. Treatment is praziquantel.'},
{t:'path',type:'sba',q:'A previously healthy 30-year-old is recovering from influenza when she develops high fever, haemoptysis and a rapidly progressive cavitating pneumonia with a low white cell count. Which organism is most likely?',
 o:['Staphylococcus aureus','Mycoplasma pneumoniae','Legionella pneumophila','Pneumocystis jirovecii','Chlamydophila psittaci'],a:0,
 e:'Influenza damages ciliated epithelium and impairs innate defences, opening the door to secondary bacterial pneumonia, especially S. aureus (sometimes Panton–Valentine leukocidin-producing strains that kill neutrophils, causing necrotising pneumonia and leukopenia) and pneumococcus. It needs urgent high-dose antibiotics.'},
{t:'path',type:'sba',q:'A man returns from Nigeria with fever and rigors 12 days after arriving home. He did not take malaria prophylaxis. What is the most important first investigation?',
 o:['Thick and thin blood films (with a rapid antigen test), repeated if negative','Sputum culture','Stool microscopy for ova','Chest X-ray only','Urine dipstick only'],a:0,
 e:'Any fever after travel to a malaria area is malaria until proven otherwise, and P. falciparum can kill within days. Thick films detect parasites; thin films identify the species and give the percentage parasitaemia (over 2% suggests severe disease). Three negative sets 12–24 hours apart are needed to exclude it.'},
{t:'path',type:'saq',q:'Six days after bloody diarrhoea from E. coli O157, a 4-year-old becomes pale, bruised and stops passing urine. Blood tests show haemolytic anaemia with fragmented red cells, low platelets and a high creatinine. Name the syndrome.',acc:['haemolyticuraemic|hemolyticuremic|hus'],m:'Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS)',
 e:'Shiga toxin enters the blood and damages glomerular endothelium, causing microthrombi that shear red cells (schistocytes), consume platelets and injure the kidney. It is the commonest cause of acute kidney injury in children. Antibiotics may increase toxin release and are generally avoided; treatment is supportive, often with dialysis.'},
/* ---------- diagnosis ---------- */
{t:'diag',type:'sba',q:'Before starting anti-TNF treatment, a patient has an interferon-γ release assay (IGRA). What does this test measure?',
 o:['IFN-γ released by the patient\'s T cells when stimulated with M. tuberculosis-specific antigens','Antibodies against M. tuberculosis','Mycobacterial DNA in blood','The size of a skin reaction to tuberculin','The number of acid-fast bacilli in sputum'],a:0,
 e:'IGRAs (e.g. QuantiFERON) expose blood to ESAT-6 and CFP-10, proteins made by M. tuberculosis but not by the BCG vaccine strain, so previous BCG does not cause a false positive (unlike the Mantoux test). A positive result shows T cell memory (latent or active TB) but cannot distinguish the two, and can be falsely negative in severe immunosuppression.'},
{t:'diag',type:'sba',q:'Two weeks after successful treatment for pneumococcal pneumonia, a PCR test on a respiratory sample is still positive for Streptococcus pneumoniae. What is the most likely explanation?',
 o:['PCR detects DNA from dead or non-viable bacteria, so it can stay positive after cure','The antibiotic has failed','PCR is insensitive, so the result must be wrong','The patient has developed antibodies to the test','PCR detects only living, dividing bacteria'],a:0,
 e:'Nucleic acid amplification tests are highly sensitive and specific but cannot tell live from dead organisms, and they do not give antibiotic sensitivities. A positive result must be interpreted with the clinical picture. Culture confirms viable organisms and allows sensitivity testing.'},
{t:'diag',type:'saq',q:'Name the mass spectrometry technique that identifies bacteria and fungi from a colony within minutes by their protein "fingerprint".',acc:['maldi'],m:'MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry',
 e:'Matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionisation time-of-flight measures the mass spectrum of ribosomal and other proteins, which is compared with a reference library. It has replaced many biochemical tests, but still needs an organism to grow first, and sensitivity testing still takes another day.'},
/* ---------- antimicrobials ---------- */
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'A patient\'s notes say "penicillin allergy". When asked, she says amoxicillin gave her diarrhoea as a child. She now has a severe chest infection. What is the best approach?',
 o:['Record it as an intolerance, not an allergy; a penicillin can be used','Avoid all β-lactams for life','Use meropenem, as it never cross-reacts','Desensitise her before any antibiotic','Use ciprofloxacin, as it is always preferred'],a:0,
 e:'About 10% of patients carry a penicillin allergy label, but most are not truly allergic: diarrhoea and nausea are side effects. True allergy means an immediate reaction (urticaria, angioedema, anaphylaxis) or a severe delayed one (e.g. Stevens–Johnson). Wrong labels push patients onto broader drugs with more C. difficile, MRSA and worse outcomes. Allergy history-taking and de-labelling are part of stewardship.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'Flucloxacillin, rather than amoxicillin, is used for Staphylococcus aureus cellulitis. Why?',
 o:['Most S. aureus produce penicillinase (a β-lactamase), and flucloxacillin is not broken down by it','Amoxicillin cannot cross the S. aureus outer membrane','S. aureus lacks penicillin-binding proteins','Amoxicillin is bacteriostatic','Flucloxacillin acts on the 30S ribosome'],a:0,
 e:'Over 80% of S. aureus make penicillinase, which opens the β-lactam ring of penicillin and amoxicillin. Flucloxacillin has a bulky side chain that blocks the enzyme. MRSA is resistant to flucloxacillin through a different mechanism: the altered PBP2a (mecA). Gram-positives have no outer membrane.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'A 30-year-old is prescribed doxycycline for Lyme disease. Which advice is correct?',
 o:['Take it with a full glass of water while sitting or standing, avoid antacids and milk around doses, and protect your skin from sun','Take it lying down after milk to reduce stomach upset','It is safe in pregnancy and in children under 8','Alcohol must be strictly avoided because of a disulfiram reaction','Expect orange urine'],a:0,
 e:'Tetracyclines can lodge in the oesophagus and cause ulceration; they chelate calcium, magnesium, aluminium and iron, which blocks absorption; and they cause photosensitivity. They bind to developing teeth and bone, so are avoided in pregnancy and in children under 12. The disulfiram reaction is metronidazole; orange urine is rifampicin.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'A 50-year-old taking sertraline is started on linezolid for an MRSA infection. Two days later he is agitated and sweating, with tremor, clonus and a temperature of 38.7 °C. What has happened?',
 o:['Serotonin syndrome: linezolid is a weak monoamine oxidase inhibitor','Neuroleptic malignant syndrome','Red man syndrome','Malignant hyperthermia','A disulfiram-like reaction'],a:0,
 e:'Linezolid reversibly inhibits monoamine oxidase, so combined with SSRIs and other serotonergic drugs it can cause serotonin syndrome (autonomic instability, neuromuscular hyperactivity with clonus, altered mental state). Linezolid also causes bone marrow suppression after about 2 weeks and optic neuropathy with long courses.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'An 84-year-old woman with an eGFR of 25 mL/min has a lower urinary tract infection. Why should nitrofurantoin be avoided?',
 o:['It is poorly concentrated in the urine at low eGFR, so it does not work, and it accumulates and causes toxicity','It is nephrotoxic like gentamicin','It always causes tendon rupture in older people','It interacts with calcium in the urine','It is only active against Gram-positive bacteria'],a:0,
 e:'Nitrofurantoin works by reaching high concentrations in urine. With poor kidney function, urinary levels are inadequate and systemic levels rise, increasing peripheral neuropathy, liver and lung toxicity. The BNF advises avoiding it when eGFR is below 45 (short courses may be used with caution between 30 and 44). Alternatives depend on local guidelines and sensitivities.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'Why is tuberculosis treated with four drugs at first rather than one?',
 o:['Resistance mutations to each drug arise independently, so a bacterium is very unlikely to resist all of them at once','The drugs must be combined to be absorbed','One drug alone works but four is cheaper','Each drug only works in one organ','The combination reduces side effects'],a:0,
 e:'A large TB burden contains spontaneous mutants resistant to any single drug (about 1 in 10⁶–10⁸ for each). Using several drugs together kills these, preventing resistance. The drugs also act on different populations: rifampicin on dormant bacilli, pyrazinamide on bacilli in acidic environments, isoniazid on rapidly dividing ones. Directly observed therapy is used when adherence is a concern.'},
{t:'drug',type:'saq',q:'Which first-line TB drug can cause optic neuritis, so that visual acuity and colour vision are checked before starting?',acc:['ethambutol'],m:'Ethambutol',
 e:'Ethambutol inhibits arabinosyl transferase (mycobacterial cell wall synthesis). Its dose-dependent optic neuritis first affects red–green colour vision and is usually reversible if the drug is stopped early. Doses are reduced in kidney impairment.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'A woman stable on warfarin is prescribed miconazole oral gel for oral thrush. A week later her INR is 9 and she has bruising. What is the mechanism?',
 o:['Miconazole inhibits CYP2C9, which metabolises warfarin, so warfarin levels rise','Miconazole contains vitamin K antagonists','Miconazole displaces warfarin from albumin permanently','Miconazole causes thrombocytopenia','Miconazole induces warfarin metabolism'],a:0,
 e:'Azoles inhibit fungal CYP51 but also human CYP450 enzymes. Oral miconazole gel is absorbed enough to inhibit CYP2C9, which metabolises the more potent S-warfarin. The MHRA has warned about this interaction. Fluconazole does the same. Rifampicin is the classic inducer that has the opposite effect.'},
{t:'drug',type:'sba',q:'Amphotericin B is highly effective against invasive fungal infection but is nephrotoxic. Which property explains its toxicity to human cells?',
 o:['It binds ergosterol strongly but also binds cholesterol in human membranes, forming pores','It inhibits human CYP450 enzymes','It is converted to 5-fluorouracil in human cells','It blocks the human β-glucan synthase','It is an aminoglycoside'],a:0,
 e:'Amphotericin forms channels in membranes containing sterols. Its affinity for ergosterol is higher than for cholesterol, which gives some selectivity, but the kidney tubule cells are damaged, causing loss of potassium and magnesium and reduced kidney function. Lipid (liposomal) formulations reduce toxicity. Flucytosine is the drug converted to 5-FU.'},
{t:'drug',type:'saq',q:'Which class of antifungals, given IV for candidaemia, inhibits β-1,3-glucan synthase in the fungal cell wall?',acc:['echinocandin|fungin'],m:'Echinocandins (caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin)',
 e:'Because humans have no cell wall, echinocandins have few side effects. They are fungicidal against most Candida species and are first line for candidaemia; they are fungistatic against Aspergillus. They are not absorbed orally and do not reach the CSF or urine well.'},
{t:'drug',type:'saq',q:'Before giving primaquine to clear the dormant liver stages of Plasmodium vivax, which enzyme deficiency must be tested for?',acc:['g6pd|glucose6phosphate'],m:'Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency',
 e:'Primaquine (and tafenoquine) produce oxidative stress. Red cells lacking G6PD cannot regenerate NADPH and reduced glutathione, so they haemolyse, causing anaemia and dark urine (with Heinz bodies and bite cells on the film). G6PD deficiency is X-linked and common in people of African, Mediterranean and Asian ancestry.'},
/* ---------- infection control, sepsis and fever ---------- */
{t:'ipc',type:'sba',q:'According to the WHO "5 moments", which moment of hand hygiene applies immediately before inserting a urinary catheter?',
 o:['Before a clean or aseptic procedure','Before touching a patient','After body fluid exposure risk','After touching a patient','After touching patient surroundings'],a:0,
 e:'Moment 2, before a clean or aseptic procedure, protects the patient from organisms (including their own) entering through a device or breach. Catheter-associated UTI is one of the commonest healthcare-associated infections; aseptic non-touch technique and removing catheters as soon as possible reduce it.'},
{t:'ipc',type:'sba',q:'A 68-year-old with pneumonia has a respiratory rate of 28, BP 85/50, heart rate 118, new confusion and a lactate of 4.5 mmol/L. As part of the first hour of sepsis care, which pair of treatments should be given?',
 o:['IV fluid bolus and IV broad-spectrum antibiotics after taking blood cultures','Oral antibiotics and repeat observations in 4 hours','IV fluids only, waiting for culture results before antibiotics','Paracetamol and a chest X-ray only','IV steroids and antihistamines'],a:0,
 e:'This is high-risk sepsis (NEWS2 high, hypotension, confusion, lactate above 4). The Sepsis Six: senior review, oxygen to target, blood cultures and bloods including lactate, IV antibiotics within 1 hour, IV fluid resuscitation and monitoring urine output. Hypotension despite fluids or a lactate of 4 or more needs critical care input.'},
{t:'ipc',type:'sba',q:'At the start of a fever a patient feels cold, shivers and has pale, cool skin, even though her temperature is 38.6 °C. Why?',
 o:['Her hypothalamic set point has risen above her current temperature, so heat-gaining responses are switched on','Her thermoregulation has failed completely','She is losing heat faster than she can make it','Peripheral cold receptors have been destroyed by the infection','She has hyperthermia with a normal set point'],a:0,
 e:'Cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-α) induce COX-2 and PGE₂ in the hypothalamus, raising the set point. Until body temperature catches up, the body behaves as though it is cold: vasoconstriction, shivering (rigors if rapid), and seeking warmth. When the set point falls (cause treated or antipyretic), she will feel hot and sweat.'}
]};
